course: dictionary
lesson: 8786
title: Shamefully and how it is used
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Shamefully is an adverb. An adverb tells you how something was done. Here it tells you how someone was treated or how someone behaved.
explain: «They were shamefully treated.» The words around it are the clue: being treated badly and unfairly is something that should make people feel ashamed. So shamefully means very badly or unfairly. Check it by swapping in very badly: the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Shamefully can also describe how a character acts. If someone behaves shamefully, they should feel sorry and put things right. In a story, Toad behaves shamefully, and later he promises to change.
explain: The Rat lists what happened to Toad: «handcuffed, imprisoned, starved, chased, terrified out of your life, insulted, jeered at, and ignominiously flung into the water—by a woman, too!» Ignominiously is a harder word that means shamefully. The list of bad things is the clue that tells you so.
fact: Shamefully means in a way that should make someone feel ashamed, or very badly or unfairly.
fact: «Shamefully» can also mean showing shame.
fact: «Ignominiously» means shamefully, so the Rat's list is a clue to its meaning.
fact: Adverbs often end in -ly and tell you how, when or where something happens.
fact: The comparative forms are more shamefully and most shamefully.
exercise: «They were shamefully treated.» What does shamefully mean here? | Quickly and quietly | Very happily | With a big smile | *In a way that should make someone feel ashamed
exercise: The Rat lists what happened to Toad. Which word in his list means the same as shamefully? | *ignominiously | chased | handcuffed | starved
exercise: In a story, Toad behaves shamefully. What should he do next? | *Feel sorry and put things right | Laugh and do it again | Forget it and never think of it | Ask someone else to take the blame
test: The bully had treated the new girl shamefully. What does shamefully mean? | With a loud laugh | Very unfairly | *Very unfairly, in a way that should make the bully ashamed | Very slowly
test: After he behaved shamefully, the boy knew what he had to do. What should he do? | Behave shamefully again | Pretend nothing happened | *Say sorry and put things right | Hide until everyone had forgotten
exam: The team played shamefully, cheating when no one was looking. What does shamefully mean here? | *In a way that should make them feel ashamed | In the pouring rain | With great joy | Slowly and carefully
